                                    className: `framer-styles-preset-17ydyq9`,
                                    "data-styles-preset": `vJYEnMnRI`,
                                    dir: `auto`,
                                    style: { "--framer-text-alignment": `justify` },
                                    children: `As previously mentioned, the approach of level designing and story-writing simultaneously causes an interesting dynamic in the way I design the difficulty curve. There are moments where the difficulty might spike up due to certain events in the story. Something like this happened when designing some of the later stages in the game. Essentially, the story will have a moment of resolve after the climax of the story, which causes the difficulty to be significantly easier than the previous one. Several aspects of the story that causes some cats to not appear in certain levels also have major effects on the difficulty curve since it can reduce the complexity of the puzzle. In order to solve this issue, I decided to add more complexity in the rules instead where one cat may have multiple specific preferences that clashes with the other. However, on the last couple levels — during the "moment of resolve" I mentioned earlier — I decided to embrace the decrease in difficulty as a way to synchronize the story cooling down with the players' experience with the difficulty as well.`,
